00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Hi. In this video, we'll be looking at testing for SSRF with Burp Suite.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Server-side request forgery is a web security vulnerability that allows an attacker to induce the server-side application to make requests to an unintended location.

3
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:29,000
These vulnerabilities may enable you to communicate with back-end systems that are not normally publicly available via a compromised server.

4
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
This is often done via non-routable private IP addresses.

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:40,000
You can use Burp Intruder to help enumerate these IPs and potentially gain access to the back-end systems.

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Before you test for SSRF, you need to identify a suitable attack vector.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
For example, this could be a request with a parameter that contains a full or partial URL.

8
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:58,000
To learn more about identifying SSRF attack surface, have a look through the Finding Hidden Attack Surface for SSRF Vulnerabilities

9
